course: reflection
lesson: 7
title: Chased, trapped, frightened: scary moments that come back at night
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Some scary moments come back at night like a film you can't switch off: the shout, the smoke, the bang, played again in the dark with a "what if" stuck on the end.
explain: Kai was chased home by three older boys shouting that they would batter him (beat him up); they didn't catch him, but in bed he thought, "They know where I live now. What if they're waiting tomorrow?"
explain: The next day he faked a tummy ache, and for two weeks he took long secret detours that made him feel hunted; the fear was real and sensible, because being chased is real danger, but hiding it alone made it grow.
explain: When he told Mum, she talked to the school and the boys were spoken to; grown-ups can do things a child can't, and walking home with a friend made the street feel his again, even if he still glanced over his shoulder for a while.
explain: Knowing what to do next time, like the family fire plan or the emergency number, turns "I was helpless" into "I'd know what to do", and that is one of the best ways to quieten a scary memory.
fact: Fear after being chased or threatened is the body doing its job; hiding it alone usually makes it grow.
fact: Grown-ups can do things a child can't, like talking to a school, other parents or the police, so a threat should be told to a trusted adult the same day.
fact: After a fire the brain can stay on alert; making a family fire plan and testing the smoke alarm together help the fear come down.
fact: The fire safety rule is "get out, stay out, call the fire service"; the emergency number is 999 in the UK, 911 in the US and Canada, 000 in Australia and 112 across Europe.
fact: Seeing a parent collapse is very scary; knowing how to call the emergency number and what to say helps children feel less helpless.
fact: Paramedics are the trained helpers who come in an ambulance.
fact: Fairground rides are checked often and are very safe, but a fright like being stuck at the top can stick for weeks.
fact: A seizure is a sudden burst of electrical activity in the brain that can make the body shake; epilepsy is a condition that can cause seizures, and people usually recover afterwards.
fact: Getting hurt in front of people adds embarrassment to pain; flinching when a ball comes near is normal for a while afterwards.
fact: A "hit and run" is when someone hurts you and leaves; grown-ups can report it, and the unfairness often hurts as much as the scrape.
fact: Many scary online videos are hoaxes, fake stories made to frighten kids and get views; they say things like "your town" to feel real.
fact: Locking someone in a dark place "as a joke" is not a joke if they panic; claustrophobia is a fear of small, closed spaces.
fact: Waiting for news about someone in hospital is very hard; most operations go fine, and the fear comes from love.
exercise: Kai was chased by three older boys who said they'd batter him. Why was his fear sensible? | Being chased is real danger, and fear is the body doing its job | It wasn't sensible; older boys never mean what they say, they just like shouting | Fear is only sensible if you actually get hurt | He was scared only because he is younger than them
exercise: What helped Kai the most? | Telling Mum the same day, so the school could step in | Taking long secret routes home for months so they couldn't find him | Faking a tummy ache every day to stay off school | Planning to get even with the boys by himself
exercise: After a small kitchen fire, Zoe kept sniffing for smoke at night. What helped her? | Making a family fire plan and testing the smoke alarm together | Checking that the cooker was switched off ten times every night before bed | Never going into the kitchen ever again | Sleeping in the garden in case of another fire
exercise: What is the fire safety rule? | Get out, stay out, call the fire service | Stay inside and hide under the bed till it's out | Go back in quickly to fetch your toys and pets | Open every window wide and wait inside for help
exercise: Oscar saw his dad collapse and is scared it will happen again when he's alone. What helped? | Learning to call the emergency number and what to say | Never leaving his dad's side, day or night, for months | Worrying about it alone every night in bed | Being told not to think about it ever again
exercise: Mia got stuck at the top of a funfair ride for twenty minutes. What is true about rides? | They are checked often and very safe, but a fright can stick | Rides get stuck because nobody ever checks them | Every ride that stops at the top like that is just about to fall down | Being stuck once means you'll be stuck every time
exercise: Leo's friend had a seizure in class and Leo thought he was dying. What is a seizure? | A burst of electrical activity in the brain that can cause shaking | A heart attack that only ever happens to children while they are at school | A kind of tummy bug that you can catch from a friend | A way of fainting that happens when you are bored
exercise: What helped Leo after his friend's seizure? | The nurse explaining, and his friend telling him how it feels | Whispering about it with other kids at break | Staying away from his friend for weeks in case the seizure was catching | Pretending he never saw anything happen
exercise: Ruby got hit in the face by a ball at PE and her nose bled in front of everyone. What is normal afterwards? | Flinching when a ball comes near for a while | Never being able to play any sport ever again | Bleeding every time someone looks at her face | Being scared of the PE teacher for ever and ever
exercise: A cyclist knocked Alfie over on the pavement and rode off shouting. What helped? | Telling Mum, who reported it, and getting plasters and a hug | Replaying it again and again in his head at night | Shouting at every single cyclist he sees on the pavement from now on | Keeping it secret so nobody would make a fuss
exercise: Grace saw a scary clown video saying it would come to "her town". What is true? | Scary videos like that are often hoaxes made for views | If a video names your town, then it must be true and the clown is coming | Clowns really do visit every town that's named | She should watch more of them to check
exercise: Jake's older cousin locked him in a dark shed "as a joke" for ten minutes. What is true? | It's not a joke if someone panics, and telling a grown-up is right | It was just a laugh between cousins, so Jake should stop moaning and get over it | Jake should lock his cousin in the shed to get even | Dark sheds are only scary to very small children
exercise: Isla's mum is in hospital for an operation, and Isla is scared she won't wake up. What is true? | Most operations go fine, and the fear comes from love | Most operations go wrong, so the fear is right and she should expect the worst | She should search online for scary operation stories | Children are not allowed to worry about parents
exercise: What helped Isla while she waited? | Calling Mum before and after, and drawing a get-well card | Googling scary stories about operations so she would know what might happen | Staying in bed until Mum got home | Pretending not to care what happened
test: Kai's mate goes, "if you grass on them they'll batter you worse, keep it shut." ("Grass" means tell on someone.) What's the better choice? | Tell a trusted adult the same day, because grown-ups can stop it | Keep quiet and take secret long routes home forever so they never catch him | Fight all three boys alone tomorrow | Stay home from school until they leave
test: What would happen if Zoe's family never tested their smoke alarm? | It might not work when they really needed it | It would work even better because it has rested | Fires would never happen in their house at all | The alarm would quietly test itself every day
test: Oscar's American cousin says, "if it happens again just call 999, dude." Oscar lives in Ohio. What number should he call? | 911, the emergency number in the US | 999, because it works the same everywhere | 112, because Ohio uses the European number | 000, because that's the number for all kids
test: In a cartoon, a boy gets stuck at the top of a big wheel and it rolls away down the street. What's the truth? | Real rides are checked a lot and are very safe | Big wheels often roll away when they stop at the top | Rides only ever stop when they are about to break | Getting stuck once means the whole ride is broken
test: A kid at school says, "Leo's mate had a fit, don't go near him, you'll catch it." What's true? | Seizures aren't catching; they come from the brain itself | It's true, seizures spread from person to person just like a cold does | It's true, but only if you touch him | Seizures only happen to people who eat sweets
test: A boy calls Ruby "bloody nose" after her nosebleed. What helped her more? | Friends checking on her and the teacher calling her brave | Calling the boy a worse name back | Hiding at home until everyone forgot | Laughing along so nobody saw she was hurt
test: Grace's older sister, Gen Z: "babe, that clown vid is literally a hoax, it's giving clickbait." ("Hoax" means a fake story.) What helped Grace? | Mum showing her how fake videos work and blocking them | Watching every clown video to see if it's real | Sleeping with the lights on every night forever | Telling her friends the clown is coming
test: Jake's aunt says, "Ah, he was only messing." What's wrong with that? | A "joke" isn't a joke if the one inside panics | Nothing; messing about can never really hurt anyone | Jake should laugh too, because it was funny | It was only wrong because the shed was dirty
test: Alfie's nan, Black Country: "Ar, it weren't yer fault, bab, 'e should've stopped." ("Bab" means dear.) Is she right? | Yes; the cyclist should have stopped, so reporting was right | No; Alfie should say sorry to the cyclist | No; it is always the walker's fault | Yes, so Alfie should chase him down himself
test: Isla thinks, "If I'm scared, something bad must be about to happen." Which is true? | Being scared shows she loves Mum; it doesn't predict what happens | Fear is a sign that bad things are definitely coming | If she stops being scared the operation will fail | Fear can make an operation go wrong
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the appropriate response of a child who has been pursued and threatened? | Disclosure to a trusted adult on the same day | Concealment of the incident and the adoption of secret routes | Retaliation against the aggressors without assistance | Permanent absence from school pending their departure
exam: Identify the principal instruction applicable in the event of a domestic fire. | Evacuate the premises, remain outside, and summon the fire service | Remain within the premises and conceal oneself beneath furniture | Re-enter the premises in order to recover personal possessions | Open all windows and await developments within
exam: It is given that a classmate has suffered a seizure. Select the response consistent with the established facts. | A seizure arises from a sudden burst of electrical activity within the brain | A seizure is a contagious condition transmitted by physical contact | A seizure invariably results in the death of the person affected | A seizure is a deliberate act performed in order to gain attention
exam: Ascertain which of the following most accurately characterises a video asserting that a costumed figure shall visit the viewer's town. | It is commonly a fabrication designed to frighten and to attract views | It is invariably an authentic warning issued by the police | It is a reliable prediction where the town is named | It is a matter which may only be verified by repeated viewing
